{
  "long_summary": "YouTube is updating its Connected TV experience to let viewers control TV playback using their phones without needing to share a Wi-Fi network or manually pair devices. The update syncs phone and TV access to engagement features like comments, likes, descriptions, and channel subscriptions, which are cumbersome to manage with a standard remote. Users can also browse other parts of the YouTube app—like searching videos or watching Shorts—while content plays on the TV, and can send videos to their TV directly from their phone's homepage feed via a "Play on different device" option in the three-dot menu. This builds on YouTube's recent CTV push, which has included podcast viewing, account locking, and channel-based "station" streams. It also extends AI-powered search capabilities, previously limited to smart TVs with microphone-equipped remotes, to a broader base of users. YouTube reports more than one billion daily hours of viewing on TV screens, making CTV a key growth area the company continues to invest in.",
  "short_summary": "YouTube now lets phones control connected TVs without shared Wi-Fi or manual pairing, syncing access to comments, likes, subscriptions, and search as daily TV viewing surpasses one billion hours."
}
```

